  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 313 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nUpper level low pressure will track across south central \nCanada, from Saskatchewan to Manitoba on Friday. A surface \ntrough of low pressure in association with this system, will \nslide across the forecast area on Friday shifting winds from the\nwest to northwest during the afternoon. Temperatures will \nremain fairly mild across the area with highs generally in the \n50s for Friday. Gusty northwesterly winds will remain in place \nbehind the surface trough Friday night and, with winds expected \nto persist into the overnight hours, lows Saturday morning will \nbe mild across the area with readings in the lower to middle \n30s. An approaching jet streak and enhanced mid level \nfrontogenesis will spread across the forecast area from \nnorthwest to southeast Saturday into Saturday night, increasing \nthe threat for precipitation across the area. ATTM, the \nstrongest lift traverses the forecast area Saturday afternoon \ninto Saturday evening. Looking at forecast soundings, \ntemperatures appear to be warm enough Saturday to support all \nrain for most areas. However, temperatures do cool enough to \nsupport rain or snow in the northwestern forecast area Saturday \nafternoon into Saturday evening. Ground temperatures across \nnorthwestern Nebraska are in the middle to upper 30s per UNL \nMesonet data. If snow rates are decent enough, we may see some \nlight accumulations in NW areas by Sunday morning. The latest \nNBM ensemble forecast only has a 20 percent chance of a 1\" snow \nover Sheridan and far western Cherry County (24hr accum.-ending \nat 6 AM Sunday morning). Since we are too far out in the \nforecast time frame to include accumulations in the forecast, \nwill probably mention this in the hwo for heightened awareness. \nLooking at the QPF forecast for the area, Saturday into Saturday\nnight, the latest NBM ensemble forecast has a 50 to 65% chance \nfor 24 hour QPF reaching 0.10\" (ending at 6 AM Sunday) along a \nswath from Sheridan County, east-southeast to Custer County and \nthe eastern Sandhills. When the threshold is raised to 0.20\" the\nprobabilities are in the 30 to 45% range. That being said, feel\nlikely pops are warranted here. As for PTYPE's, the forecast \nbuilder initialized with rain or snow mix for Saturday night. \nGiven the low probabilities for snow accumulations, feel this is\nthe way to go for the day 4 forecast. After Saturday night, low\namplitude ridging will build into the Central Plains Sunday \nnight into Monday. This will lead to dry conditions and highs \nMonday in the mid 40s to lower 50s. Low amplitude flow will \ncontinue into Tuesday with a continuation of dry conditions and \nhighs reaching the middle 40s to middle 50s.",
